00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:00,720
All right.

2
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:03,707
So then when it's time to step up and speak up,

3
00:00:03,707 --> 00:00:05,811
what do we do in that very moment?

4
00:00:05,811 --> 00:00:09,060
That's about what you say, where you say it,

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,625
how you say it, with what emotional tone.

6
00:00:11,625 --> 00:00:15,086
And I'll give just one specific, sort of, general piece

7
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:16,084
of advice here.

8
00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:19,087
All of us when asked or when thinking about, like,

9
00:00:19,087 --> 00:00:20,800
I'm gonna go for it on this issue,

10
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:26,115
our first instinct is going to be to say the matter,

11
00:00:26,115 --> 00:00:29,251
present the issue, try to give the persuasive remarks

12
00:00:29,251 --> 00:00:31,904
from the perspective that's compelling to us.

13
00:00:31,904 --> 00:00:33,107
I mean, after all, right,

14
00:00:33,107 --> 00:00:36,590
I mean, it's our brain in which we're concocting the story,

15
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:38,140
the argument, the pitch.

16
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:40,849
And so our tendency is gonna be to frame it in a way

17
00:00:40,849 --> 00:00:41,752
that works for us.

18
00:00:41,752 --> 00:00:45,122
Often that's exactly wrong, because if you already controlled,

19
00:00:45,122 --> 00:00:48,379
right, the behavior of the other person or the resources the

20
00:00:48,379 --> 00:00:51,468
other person controls, you don't need to do this anyway.

21
00:00:51,468 --> 00:00:57,224
And so imagine, for example, that I work for you or with you,

22
00:00:57,224 --> 00:01:03,459
and you are really compelled by things that affect us economically,

23
00:01:03,459 --> 00:01:05,857
that hit the bottom line,

24
00:01:05,857 --> 00:01:10,451
and you really are sensitive to threats or risks to our well-being

25
00:01:10,451 --> 00:01:11,511
or performance.

26
00:01:11,511 --> 00:01:14,681
So you care about the money and you care about threats.

27
00:01:14,681 --> 00:01:17,760
But I come in, pitch in this great new idea to you,

28
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,370
and I'm talking about how it fits with our values and is so

29
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:25,478
culturally aligned with who we are and how it's such an opportunity.

30
00:01:25,478 --> 00:01:28,590
And that opportunity, framing and cultural framing

31
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:30,139
doesn't resonate for you at all,

32
00:01:30,139 --> 00:01:33,656
because I failed to mention the economic reality or the potential

33
00:01:33,656 --> 00:01:35,194
threats if we don't do this.

34
00:01:35,194 --> 00:01:39,178
And so people have to remember that it's the target's ability to

35
00:01:39,178 --> 00:01:42,529
hear and respond well to what you're saying that makes

36
00:01:42,529 --> 00:01:43,731
all the difference.

37
00:01:43,731 --> 00:01:47,037
And my book talks about lots and lots of specific strategies

38
00:01:47,037 --> 00:01:48,101
for achieving that.

39
00:01:48,101 --> 00:01:51,933
But the high level concept is you got to speak to the target.

40
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:54,833
And then as we started with, you know, I mentioned the importance

41
00:01:54,833 --> 00:01:57,283
of following up, whether things have gone well and

42
00:01:57,283 --> 00:02:00,234
you're securing additional resources or timelines or whether

43
00:02:00,234 --> 00:02:02,684
they haven't gone so well and you're trying

44
00:02:02,684 --> 00:02:03,434
to mend fences.

45
00:02:03,434 --> 00:02:05,390
That's really important too.

46
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:07,966
And so then with -- when it comes to the framing,

47
00:02:07,966 --> 00:02:11,362
I would like to hear some of these specific tidbits there.

48
00:02:11,362 --> 00:02:15,465
So do you have some archetypes or categories of frames,

49
00:02:15,465 --> 00:02:19,076
or values, economic? Those sounded nice in terms of,

50
00:02:19,076 --> 00:02:20,899
yeah, those have very different flavors to them.

51
00:02:20,899 --> 00:02:22,341
Any others that come to mind?

52
00:02:22,341 --> 00:02:24,495
So there are some other, sort of, broader frames,

53
00:02:24,495 --> 00:02:30,095
for example, I versus we or, sort of, win win versus win loss.

54
00:02:30,095 --> 00:02:33,985
I think what we often fail to remember,

55
00:02:33,985 --> 00:02:37,884
we know this, but we fail to remember it in the moment, is that

56
00:02:37,884 --> 00:02:41,463
when you're telling somebody why they should do something

57
00:02:41,463 --> 00:02:44,019
differently or you're pitching your idea,

58
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:47,278
part of what they're hearing is -- as the recipient is,

59
00:02:47,278 --> 00:02:48,940
oh, you're saying I'm bad

60
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:54,042
or my idea or current practice is inferior or,

61
00:02:54,042 --> 00:02:57,938
oh, you wanna do this, so you look good and I look like a fool.

62
00:02:57,938 --> 00:03:04,858
And so framing that helps people understand I don't want to replace

63
00:03:04,858 --> 00:03:07,270
or win at your expense,

64
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:11,057
I want to take what you've done to the next level.

65
00:03:11,057 --> 00:03:14,697
I wanna be the scout out front who then brings us all

66
00:03:14,697 --> 00:03:15,708
along together.

67
00:03:15,708 --> 00:03:19,398
I wanna expand the pie for everyone so that helping people be

68
00:03:19,398 --> 00:03:23,148
able to hear what you're saying because they really think you're

69
00:03:23,148 --> 00:03:26,779
on their side and that you're advancing excellence rather than

70
00:03:26,779 --> 00:03:28,148
beating something down.

71
00:03:28,148 --> 00:03:30,780
That's a huge element of positive framing.

